---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import SwitchBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/SwitchBuild.astro"
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components"
---

<TechniquePost slug="switch-checkbox">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A switch is a checkbox that reads as on and off. Libraries tend to build
      it from a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> with <code>aria-checked</code> toggled
      in JavaScript, or a hidden checkbox plus extra spans for the track and the dot.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both throw away what the checkbox already does: toggling, Space to press,
      {" "}<code>:checked</code>, a form value and validation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>&lt;input type="checkbox" role="switch"&gt;</code> keeps the native
        behavior, the role makes assistive technology announce on and off
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>appearance: none</code> frees the input to draw its own pseudo-elements:
        {" "}<code>::before</code> is the track, <code>::after</code> is the dot
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>:checked</code> moves the dot with <code>inset-inline-start</code
        >: track width minus dot minus gap, mirrored in right-to-left
      </li>
      <li>
        An <code>outline</code> in the dot's own color grows it without touching its
        box. <code>:active</code> grows it a little more
      </li>
      <li><code>light-dark()</code> picks the colors per color scheme</li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`[role="switch"] {
  appearance: none;
  position: relative;
}

[role="switch"]::after {
  background-color: var(--dot-color);
  border-radius: var(--radius-round);
  content: "";
  inset-inline-start: var(--size-1);
  outline: var(--dot-outline-size) solid var(--dot-color);
  outline-offset: -1px;
  position: absolute;
  transition: all 0.2s var(--ease);
}

[role="switch"]:checked::after {
  --dot-outline-size: calc(var(--size-1) - 1px);

  inset-inline-start: calc(var(--track-width) - var(--dot-size) - var(--size-1));
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <SwitchBuild />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        There is no <code>@supports</code>: <code>appearance: none</code>,
        pseudo-elements on a checkbox and logical insets work in every current
        engine
      </li>
      <li>
        The colors come from <code>light-dark()</code>. A browser without it
        can't resolve those custom properties, and the track and dot lose their
        colors
      </li>
      <li>
        Under reduced motion <code>--motion</code> is <code>0</code> and the dot jumps
        instead of sliding
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
